  "output": {
    "section_id": "ch03.open",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "ch03.open.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.open.p01.01",
            "text": "The previous chapter carried raw text through tokenization, token IDs, and embeddings; here the focus shifts to what embeddings still lack and how transformer blocks supply it.",
            "type": "R",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.open.p01.02",
            "text": "A token embedding is a vector representation in a higher-dimensional space, and these vectors capture semantic meaning, with related words lying closer together, but an embedding by itself does not say how a word relates to the other words in its sentence.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "eSRhpYLerw4@185",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@188",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@192",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@217",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@219",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@222",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@229",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@250",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@253",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@255",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@209",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@212",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@215"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.open.p01.03",
            "text": "That missing relational information matters because next-word prediction needs context, and attention is the mechanism that turns each embedding into a context vector enriched with both token meaning and sentence-level relationships.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "eSRhpYLerw4@277",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@279",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@282",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@286",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@305",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@307",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@315",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@317",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@319",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@322",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@325",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@331",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@334",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@346",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@348",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@357",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@184",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@187",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@189",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@191",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@235",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@238",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@240",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@244",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@247"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.open.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.open.p02.01",
            "text": "We begin by reconnecting tokens, embeddings, and context, then build intuition for simplified self-attention, attention scores, normalization, softmax, and context-vector computation before vectorizing the whole process over a sequence.",
            "type": "R",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.open.p02.02",
            "text": "From there, the chapter explains why trainable \"Q\", \"K\", and \"V\" projections are needed, then moves into causal masking, dropout, batching, and efficient multi-head attention implementations.",
            "type": "R",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.open.p02.03",
            "text": "It closes with the transformer blocks wrapped around attention itself, namely layer normalization and shortcut connections, while leaving fuller linear-algebra and matrix-shape intuition only partly spelled out and carrying the assembled pieces forward into the next chapter on putting a GPT model together.",
            "type": "R",
            "cites": []
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.glance.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.glance.p01.01",
            "text": "An embedding vector is a token’s vector representation in a higher-dimensional space, and embedding vectors capture semantic meaning so that semantically related words are closer together.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "eSRhpYLerw4@185",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@188",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@192",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@217",
              "eSRhpYLerw4@219",
